{"name":"Golden Syrup","hex":"#ebd8b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden syrup","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pale amber-coloured treacle produced as part of the process of refining cane sugar, used in cooking and as a spread."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden_syrup"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden syrup","blocks":[{"text":"Golden syrup or light treacle is a thick, amber-coloured form of inverted sugar syrup made by the process of refining sugar cane or sugar beet juice into sugar. It is used in a variety of baking recipes and desserts. It has an appearance and consistency similar to honey, and is often used as a substitute where honey is unavailable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Golden_Syrup.JPG/330px-Golden_Syrup.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_syrup","wikidata":"Q2688129"},{"title":"Syrup","blocks":[{"text":"In cooking, syrup is a thick, viscous, liquid condiment consisting primarily of a solution of sugar in water. It typically contains a large amount of dissolved sugars but shows little tendency to deposit crystals. In its concentrated form, its consistency is similar to that of molasses. The viscosity arises from the multiple hydrogen bonds between the dissolved sugar, which has many hydroxyl (OH) groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Maltose_syrup.jpg/330px-Maltose_syrup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrup","wikidata":"Q6584340"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Tate_and_Lyle_building_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1124019.jpg/500px-Tate_and_Lyle_building_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1124019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Tate_and_Lyle_building_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1124019.jpg/500px-Tate_and_Lyle_building_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1124019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tate & Lyle syrup refinery at Plaistow Wharf, east London, England, featuring a large-scale representation of a golden syrup can on the nearest corner","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATate_and_Lyle_building_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1124019.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lyle%27sGoldenSyrup.jpg/500px-Lyle%27sGoldenSyrup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Lyle%27sGoldenSyrup.jpg/500px-Lyle%27sGoldenSyrup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tin of Lyle's Golden Syrup, first sold in 1885. Recognised by Guinness World Records as having the world's oldest branding and packaging.[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALyle'sGoldenSyrup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/THE_WOMEN%27S_WORK_IN_THE_WAR_PRODUCTION%2C_1914-1918_Q110095.jpg/500px-THE_WOMEN%27S_WORK_IN_THE_WAR_PRODUCTION%2C_1914-1918_Q110095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/THE_WOMEN%27S_WORK_IN_THE_WAR_PRODUCTION%2C_1914-1918_Q110095.jpg/1280px-THE_WOMEN%27S_WORK_IN_THE_WAR_PRODUCTION%2C_1914-1918_Q110095.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https